# Breaking changes to the standard library's public surface, declared.
#
# `scripts/check-compat.sh` refuses a removed, retyped or
# effect-widened public name unless a line here names it against the
# version that makes the change. That is the whole enforcement: a
# break is allowed, and it is allowed only when somebody wrote down
# that they meant it.
#
# Format, whitespace-separated:
#
#   <version>  <kind-letter>  <name>  <why>
#
# The kind letter is AXSYM's: F function, D data, C constructor,
# S struct, T trait. `docs/compatibility.md` is the policy this file
# implements.

# 0.3.3 - ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3, the half of it where `-errno` genuinely
# means a failure. Seven filesystem calls and `copyFile` answer
# `(Result Int Error)` instead of an Int that is a byte count when
# positive and a negated errno when negative. See docs/error-model.md
# section 10.
0.3.3  F  writeFile   -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  appendFile  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  removeFile  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  renamePath  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  fileSize    -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  makeDirAll  -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  removeDir   -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type
0.3.3  F  copyFile    -errno -> (Result Int Error): the failure is in the type

# 0.3.4 - ERR-ADOPT-1 slice 3, the `Sys` half. Seven raw syscall
# wrappers answer `(Result Int Error)` instead of an Int that is a
# result when non-negative and a negated errno when negative.
#
# They read WIDENED rather than CHANGED because the effect row moved
# too: building an `Error` allocates, so each gained `Alloc`. The type
# changed as well - a widened row and a changed signature are the same
# event here, and the gate reports the coarser one.
0.3.4  F  sysWriteFile   -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysAppendFile  -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysUnlink      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysMkdir       -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRmdir       -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRename      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysFileSize    -errno -> (Result Int Error)
# `makeDir` went with them: its body is one `sysMkdir` call, and its
# declared `Int` return silently became a Result HANDLE - which the
# checker cannot catch, because `Int` is the universal heap-handle
# type. It printed a heap address as an errno until the fixture caught
# it.
0.3.4  F  makeDir        -errno -> (Result Int Error)
# `ioResult` now RE-WRAPS a `Sys` Result rather than converting a raw
# Int, because `Sys` converts at its own layer now.
0.3.4  F  ioResult       (Int -> ...) -> ((Result Int Error) -> ...)

# 0.3.4 - the process half of slice 3. `sysRun`'s three-way answer is
# now split by the TYPE rather than by a sign: `Err` means the child
# NEVER RAN (the spawn's own errno), `Ok` means it ran and carries what
# it answered, including 128+n for a signal. That is the distinction
# `Sys.ax`'s own comment says a driver must not lose - "could not start
# llc" against "llc rejected the module".
#
# The net calls are deliberately NOT here: `netPollWait` runs inside
# the echo server's event loop, where an `(Ok n)` block would allocate
# per poll wake outside the per-connection arena scope, and
# `check-net.sh` asserts memory ratios on exactly that server. Same
# reasoning that leaves `IO.writeStr` on a sentinel.
0.3.4  F  sysSpawn        -errno -> (Result Int Error): Err means no child
0.3.4  F  sysWaitPid      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRun          -errno -> (Result Int Error): Err means the child never ran
0.3.4  F  sysRunPath      -errno -> (Result Int Error)
0.3.4  F  sysRandomBytes  -errno -> (Result Int Error)

# 0.6.0 - dogfooding parameterised structs (landed 2026-08-31): a
# `data` type with one constructor that existed only to carry a single
# function field is now the struct that field always was. The
# constructor name `HttpFn` is gone; construct with `(HttpHandler
# (lambda (fd r) ...))` and read the field with `.run` instead of
# matching. The compare tool keys identity as (kind, name), so a `D`
# becoming an `S` reads as this name REMOVED from the `D` row - the
# `S HttpHandler` row that replaces it is an ADDED row, which needs no
# declaration of its own.
0.6.0  D  HttpHandler  data HttpHandler (ctor HttpFn) -> struct HttpHandler (field run): a one-constructor data type is a struct

# 0.6.0 - dogfooding `Option`: four sentinel functions in
# `compat/SENTINELS`'s `absence` column answer `Option Int` in place
# of a raw `-1`, per `docs/error-model.md` ERR-REC-3 ("Option is for
# absence"). Zero external callers of any of the four, checked before
# porting; every caller that needed updating lived in the same file.
0.6.0  F  axsymHexVal    -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers
0.6.0  F  axsymPctAt     -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers
0.6.0  F  pathLastSlash  -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers
0.6.0  F  pathExtIndex   -1 -> (Option Int): absence, not a sentinel; no external callers
